    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_200914c01a864df8aa2c67e8dc11115004061522d32494f55a1920a0ac89e8e0",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Averaging the original sum over shifts of product form xy creates the small parameter xy/n, so Taylor expansion replaces the logarithmic factor log(1+xy/n) by a polynomial in xy of degree r=floor(5.01 log t/log N), up to the stated averaging and truncation errors. The gain is algebraic structure; the cost is that the necessary degree grows like log t/log N, and the source identifies excessive degree relative to N as the eventual obstruction. This span does not yet estimate the resulting polynomial sum U(n), so that proof burden remains.",
