    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_9c92fa7a9576d0fcf32e6589a905ec2ef655caa322eccb5478a53978bfa82599",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Because the vectors (x,x^2,...,x^r) occupy a very sparse subset of their ambient box, direct completion would lose too much. Two applications of Hölder instead duplicate first one variable family and then the other: power-sum differences are grouped by shifted counts J_{k,r}(lambda;Z), Cauchy–Schwarz bounds every shifted count by the central count J_{k,r}(Z), and expansion finally separates the coordinates into one-dimensional reciprocal-spacing sums. The gain is a factorization of sparse-curve multiplicity from phase spacing without pretending the curve fills a box; the loss is severe dilution, since all savings pass through a final 1/(4k^2) root.",
